it might look like a river but this is  actually a paddy field  in the village of sampang in patti  regency this community of farmers in  central java  is trying to salvage their crops days of  severe flooding have destroyed most of  their hard work  and most of the rice is rotten  [Music]  look all around everything is ruined  there is no harvest  this community is used to heavy rains  but farmers told al jazeera past floods  didn't reach the paddy fields  they say land degradation is to blame  for the worsening flooding  there are many mining activities going  on uphill mining for the cement  factories forest destruction for  plantations  when it rains the water flows straight  to the juana river and it overflows  the disaster agency says seven thousand  hectares of land is flooded  and in some areas the water rose more  than a meter and a half  aside from the la nina factor we cannot  deny this flood is caused by  environmental destruction  especially in the forest environmental  groups are campaigning for a moratorium  on mining permits  they blame the worsening floods on  upstream mining for the cement factory  but local authorities in parti maintain  the floods are caused by heavy rain  these floods are because the jonah river  overflowed it happens annually when the  rainfall is high  this week severe flooding affected  thousands of households in jakarta  and nearby cities and local authorities  also blamed heavy rain  flooding in java causes millions of  dollars of damage  every year in jakarta and in smaller  cities  and authorities are often reluctant to  make the connection  between environmental damage and the  worsening floods  in patti the local government says it  will give seeds to the affected farmers  but the farmers are still concerned  if it continues like this all is lost we  can't plant again now  seeds they say will not be enough if the  bigger issues aren't addressed as well  jessica washington al jazeera jakarta
 

